WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.432
IP addressing is a topic we are going to cover in detail later in this course. As it relates to LANs, however, the vast majority of the time, LANs will have private IP addresses.

00:11.432 --> 00:27.040
Take, for example, the IP address of this device. I am in a command-line tool and I'm going to run the ipconfig command. The IP address on this adaptor starts with 10.

00:27.040 --> 00:39.202
Any IP address starting with 10 is a private IP address, meaning it is not routable over a public network, like the Internet. Here's another adaptor with a private IP address.

00:39.202 --> 00:46.870
This IP address starts with 192.168. That is another range of private IP addresses.

00:46.870 --> 00:55.055
The last range of private IP addresses you need to know about for the exam is this one: this address starts with 172.22,

00:55.055 --> 01:04.135
but any IP address that starts with the numbers 172.16 through 172.31 is a private IP address.

01:04.135 --> 01:13.397
With this addressing scheme, you will often see the same IP addresses being used on different LANs. That is the whole point of private IP addressing.

01:13.397 --> 01:21.408
These addresses can be reused, which saves from public IP addresses having to be used on every device.

